/// \fn static INIFieldParseProc findFieldParse(const FieldParse *parseTable, const char *token, int &offset, const void *&userData)
/// \brief Looks a field name up in one FieldParse table and returns the
///        parse function, the field's offset and the row's userData.
///
/// This is how a line inside a block reaches its parser. For every line,
/// INI::initFromINIMulti takes the first token, split at spaces, tabs, line
/// ends and the equals sign, as the field name. Unless it is the block end
/// token (END, compared without regard to case), it asks this function
/// about each table of the MultiIniFieldParse in turn, and calls the first
/// parser it gets back with the object being filled and the field address:
/// the object plus the returned offset plus that table's extra offset. When
/// no table yields a parser, INI::initFromINIMulti stops the load with an
/// INIException that names the unknown field and the block. When the parser
/// itself throws, it rethrows with the field, block, file and line added.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A linear scan of the rows up to the terminating row (the one with no
/// field name), comparing names with strcmp. On a match it returns that
/// row's parser and offset and its userData. If nothing matches but the
/// terminating row has a parser, that row is a catch-all: it returns that
/// parser and offset with the field name itself as userData. Otherwise it
/// returns null and leaves the out parameters untouched. Tables are neither
/// sorted nor hashed, so each line costs a scan of every table up to the
/// one that answers.
///
/// \param parseTable A FieldParse array ending in a row with no field name.
/// \param token      The field name read from the line.
/// \param offset     [out] The row's offset; written only when a parser is
///                   returned.
/// \param userData   [out] The row's userData, or for a catch-all row the
///                   field name; written only when a parser is returned.
/// \return The parse function, or null when the table neither has the name
///         nor ends in a catch-all row.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Field names must match exactly, including case: buildcost is not
///   BuildCost. A field name cannot contain a space, a tab or an equals
///   sign.
/// - Tables are tried in order and the first answer wins. A catch-all row
///   therefore takes every name its own table lacks, and tables after it
///   are never consulted; a misspelt field in such a block goes to the
///   catch-all parser instead of producing the unknown-field error.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's INI.cpp has the same static function with the same
///       rules.
/// \see INI::initFromINIMulti, INI::initFromINI

/// \fn static ScienceType INI::scanScience(const char *token)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_ini_field_callbacks
/// \brief Converts a science name into its ScienceType, stopping the load
///        when no such science is defined.
///
/// Field parsers that read science names, including
/// parsePrerequisiteScience, use it to turn each token into a science.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A one-line forwarder to ScienceStore::friend_lookupScience on
/// TheScienceStore. That function turns the name into a name key with
/// NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey (a science's type is its name key) and
/// throws an INIException reading "Science name ... not known! (Did you
/// define it in Science.ini?)" when the store has no such science.
///
/// \param token The science name.
/// \return The science's type. The function does not return for an unknown
///         name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Sciences must be loaded before any file that names one, because the name
/// is checked as the field is read. The name must be spelt as in its
/// definition, including case, since it goes through
/// NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey. Zero Hour's scanScience is the same
/// forwarder.
/// \see ScienceStore::friend_lookupScience, parsePrerequisiteScience

/// \fn static Int BitFlags::getSingleBitFromName(const char *token)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_ini_field_callbacks
/// \brief Returns the bit index whose name matches token, ignoring case,
///        or -1 when no name matches.
///
/// BitFlags is the engine's fixed-size flag set, a template over the number
/// of bits. Each instantiation has its own name table, one name per bit in
/// bit order, ending in a null entry. This lookup is how a flag name in
/// data becomes a bit: BitFlags::setBitByName calls it, and so do field
/// parsers, Lua functions and script code that take a single flag name. In
/// Zero Hour BitFlags::xfer saves the set bits by name and, when loading,
/// turns each name back into a bit through setBitByName, failing the load
/// on an unknown name; in BFME 2 the 11-bit disabled set's xfer reaches
/// this lookup the same way.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// It walks the instantiation's name table and returns the position of the
/// first name equal to token under _strcmpi, the C runtime's
/// case-insensitive compare. Retail has a separate copy for each flag set,
/// identical except for the table it reads. This page names each set by
/// the number of names in its retail table. Count the table rather than
/// reading a size from the template argument in a symbol name; in the
/// reconstruction the two need not agree.
/// - 591 names, the model condition flags (ModelConditionFlags). The table
///   runs from TOPPLED, FRONTCRUSHED, BACKCRUSHED to SPECIAL_WEAPON_SIX, and
///   the set's xfer, when saving, checks each of the 591 bits. Callers
///   include BitFlags::setBitByName, the Lua functions
///   CurDrawableModelcondition and ObjectTestModelCondition, map-script
///   code that counts or tests objects by model condition, and the parsers
///   behind keys such as CustomAnimAndDuration, ConditionForEntry,
///   SetModelCondition and AddTempConditionFlag.
/// - 154 names, special power types. The table opens with the same first
///   names as Zero Hour's list (SPECIAL_INVALID, SPECIAL_DAISY_CUTTER,
///   SPECIAL_PARADROP_AMERICA, ...) but diverges after a few entries. It
///   keeps some other Zero Hour names and includes BFME names such as
///   SPECIAL_MAN_THE_WALLS and SPECIAL_SPAWN_ORCS, so bit indices do not
///   match Zero Hour's.
/// - 104 names. The table opens VETERAN, ELITE, HERO, PLAYER_UPGRADE, as
///   Zero Hour's weapon-set and armor-set condition lists do, and includes
///   WEAPONSET_TOGGLE_1, WEAPONSET_HERO_MODE,
///   WEAPONSET_CREATE_A_HERO_WS_01 to WEAPONSET_CREATE_A_HERO_WS_64 and
///   ARMORSET_CREATE_A_HERO_01 to ARMORSET_CREATE_A_HERO_10. Which fields
///   read this set is not established here.
/// - 15 names, attribute-modifier categories, in full: NONE, LEADERSHIP,
///   FORMATION, SPELL, WEAPON, STRUCTURE, LEVEL, BUFF, DEBUFF, STUN,
///   INNATE_ARMOR, INNATE_DAMAGEMULT, INNATE_VISION, INNATE_AUTOHEAL,
///   INNATE_HEALTH. The Category key of the attribute-modifier definition
///   (whose table also holds Modifier, Duration, FX and
///   ReplaceInCategoryIfLongest) is parsed through it. The Category parser
///   accepts every name except NONE; NONE or an unknown name stops the load
///   with an "Invalid Category" error that names ModifierList::Category.
/// - 12 names, emotion types, in full: TAUNT, CHEER, HERO_CHEER, POINT,
///   FEAR, UNCONTROLLABLE_FEAR, TERROR, DOOM, QUARRELSOME, ALERT,
///   BRACE_FOR_BEING_CRUSHED, CHEER_FOR_ABOUT_TO_CRUSH. Used for the Type
///   key of the emotion definition and for EmotionType,
///   PreLandingEmotion and PostLandingEmotion keys elsewhere.
/// - 11 names, disabled types, in full: DEFAULT, DISABLED_USER_PARALYZED,
///   DISABLED_EMP, DISABLED_HELD, DISABLED_PARALYZED, DISABLED_UNMANNED,
///   DISABLED_UNDERPOWERED, DISABLED_FREEFALL, DISABLED_TEMPORARILY_BUSY,
///   DISABLED_SCRIPT_DISABLED, DISABLED_SCRIPT_UNDERPOWERED.
/// - 6 names, AI states an emotion can impose, in full: BACK_AWAY,
///   AVOID_SCARER, IDLE, RUN_AWAY_PANIC, FACE_OBJECT, QUARREL (the same list
///   as in BFME 1). The emotion definition's AIState key is parsed through
///   it.
///
/// Other sizes, such as the KindOf set, are the same function over their
/// own tables.
///
/// \param token The flag name to look up.
/// \return The bit index, from 0, or -1 when no name matches.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Flag names are matched without regard to case wherever this lookup is
///   used, so frontcrushed works as well as FRONTCRUSHED, in INI data, Lua
///   and map scripts alike.
/// - The names are compiled into the executable, and the bit count is part
///   of the type. A new flag needs a code change that grows the table and
///   the set.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Changing a table changes the meaning of bit indices everywhere the set
/// is used. Saved games store set bits by name (Zero Hour's
/// BitFlags::xfer), so renaming or removing a name breaks loading saves
/// that contain it.
/// \see BitFlags::setBitByName

/// \fn unsigned int ParseAlphaNumericBitMask(const char *description, unsigned int fallback)
/// \brief Turns a string of letters and the digits 0 to 3 into a bit mask,
///        one bit per character.
///
/// A compact way to write a set of up to 31 members as letters. No call or
/// jump to it, and no pointer to it, has been found in the retail
/// executable, so no data path that reaches it is known. BFME 1 has the
/// same function and table.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A 256-entry table maps each character to a bit:
/// - the at sign and the backquote to bit 0;
/// - A to Z and a to z to bits 1 to 26, so case does not matter;
/// - the digits 0 to 3 to bits 27 to 30;
/// - every other character to -1.
///
/// The scan sets the bit of each character in turn and stops at the first
/// character that maps to -1, which includes the end of the string, a
/// space and the digits 4 to 9. Retail sign-extends the character before
/// the table lookup, so a byte above 127 indexes before the table rather
/// than into its upper half. Retail's copy is compiled without
/// optimisation.
///
/// \param description The set as text, or null.
/// \param fallback    Returned unchanged when description is null.
/// \return The mask, or fallback for a null description. An empty string,
///         or one that starts with an unmapped character, gives 0, not the
///         fallback.

/// \fn void parseAIKindOfList(INI *ini, const char *, int *out)
/// \brief Field parser for TargetTypes: reads up to 16 AI kind names and
///        stores their indices in consecutive integers.
///
/// The TargetTypes key shares its FieldParse table with Unit, which takes a
/// single AI kind, and TargetPriorityModifiers. The AI kinds, in index
/// order, are INFANTRY, ARCHER, PIKEMAN, CAVALRY, CREEP, CREEP_STRUCTURE,
/// STRUCTURE, SIEGEWEAPON, EXPLORABLE_AREA, WALL, HERO, BATTLE_TOWER,
/// SHIP_BATTLESHIP, SHIP_BOMBARD, SHIP_TRANSPORT and SHIP_SUICIDE. No Zero
/// Hour or BFME 1 source for this parser exists in the references, so its
/// name, and getAIKindOfFromName's, were chosen by the reconstruction.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The first name is required: it is read with INI::getNextToken, which
/// throws when the line has no value. The rest are read with
/// INI::getNextTokenOrNull until the line ends, all with the default
/// separators. Each name goes through getAIKindOfFromName, which throws an
/// INIException naming an invalid AI_KINDOF for an unknown name. A
/// seventeenth name stops the load with "In an AIKINDOF list, each type may
/// only appear once"; duplicates within 16 names are not checked. The
/// parser writes neither a count nor an end marker, so entries after the
/// last name keep whatever they held before.
///
/// The FieldParse row calls it with the usual field-parser arguments; the
/// second, which receives the object being filled, is never read.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param out [out] The first of 16 integers receiving the AI kind indices.
///
/// \par Modding
/// AI kind names are matched by getAIKindOfFromName, not by this parser;
/// the list itself is fixed in the executable.
/// \see getAIKindOfFromName

/// \fn void parseGeometryRotationAnchorOffset(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for GeometryRotationAnchorOffset and
///        ClearanceGeometryRotationAnchorOffset: reads an X and Y offset
///        into a geometry description.
///
/// GeometryRotationAnchorOffset shares its field address, the object
/// definition's geometry description, with other Geometry rows of that
/// table, including Geometry, AdditionalGeometry, GeometryMajorRadius,
/// GeometryMinorRadius and GeometryOffset (not every Geometry key does:
/// GeometryOther and GeometryFrontAngle write elsewhere).
/// ClearanceGeometryRotationAnchorOffset shares its address with
/// ClearanceGeometry, ClearanceGeometryMajorRadius,
/// ClearanceGeometryMinorRadius, ClearanceGeometryHeight and
/// ClearanceGeometryIsSmall (ClearanceGeometryOffset, nearby, writes
/// elsewhere). Because those rows share one field address, this parser moves
/// to the anchor-offset member inside the description and lets
/// INI::parseCoord2D read X and Y sub-tokens into it. The name suggests the
/// point the geometry rotates about; how the offset is used is not covered
/// here.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled; passed on.
/// \param store    The geometry description the row points at.
/// \param userData Passed on.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The value is written as X:x Y:y with real numbers. The body is the same
/// as BFME 1's.
/// \see INI::parseCoord2D

/// \fn void parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for the global MultiPlayUnitXPMult key: reads
///        MPn value pairs into a per-slot table of multipliers.
///
/// One of five global-data keys that each fill a per-slot array of reals
/// in the same record: MultiPlayMoneyMult (parseMultiPlayMoneyMult),
/// MultiPlayUnitXPMult, MultiPlayBuildingXPMult, MultiPlayUnitSpeedMult
/// and MultiPlayBuildingSpeedMult. All five rows of the global data table
/// point at that record, and each parser writes its own array through a
/// small setter of the record (here MultiPlayMults::setUnitXPMult).
/// MultiPlayMults and its setter names are descriptive names taken from the
/// BFME 1 reconstruction, not recovered ones. The record's constructor sets
/// every slot to 1.0. It also has a sixth array, which none of these keys
/// fills. What a slot number selects, and what reads these arrays, is not
/// established here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Tokens are read with the colon separator set. The parser reads a slot
/// name, then requires a value (INI::getNextToken throws if the line ends)
/// and converts it with INI::scanReal. It compares the name with MP1 to MP8
/// under _strcmpi and stores the value for slot n through the setter. A
/// pair whose name is anything else is read and ignored. It repeats until
/// the line ends. The setter stores slot n at index n - 1 and ignores slots
/// outside 1 to 20, so the arrays have room for 20 slots while the parser
/// can reach only the first 8.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    The multiplier record that all five keys write to.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Write pairs such as MultiPlayUnitXPMult = MP1:1.0 MP2:1.25. Slot names
///   are not case-sensitive; any slot left out keeps its previous value
///   (1.0 unless set earlier).
/// - A misspelt slot name, or MP9 and above, is silently ignored.
///
/// \note The BFME 1 reconstruction has the same five keys with 32-entry
///       arrays; BFME 2's setters bound the slot at 20.
/// \see parseMultiPlayBuildingXPMult, parseMultiPlayUnitSpeedMult,
///      parseMultiPlayBuildingSpeedMult, parseMultiPlayMoneyMult

/// \fn void parseMultiPlayBuildingXPMult(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for the global MultiPlayBuildingXPMult key: MPn value
///        pairs into the building experience multiplier slots.
///
/// The same parser as parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult, writing the building
/// experience array through MultiPlayMults::setBuildingXPMult. MP1 to MP8
/// are accepted without regard to case; other names are ignored.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    The multiplier record that all five keys write to.
/// \param userData Not used.
/// \see parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult

/// \fn void parseMultiPlayUnitSpeedMult(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for the global MultiPlayUnitSpeedMult key: MPn value
///        pairs into the unit speed multiplier slots.
///
/// The same parser as parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult, writing the unit speed
/// array through MultiPlayMults::setUnitSpeedMult. MP1 to MP8 are accepted
/// without regard to case; other names are ignored.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    The multiplier record that all five keys write to.
/// \param userData Not used.
/// \see parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult

/// \fn void parseMultiPlayBuildingSpeedMult(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for the global MultiPlayBuildingSpeedMult key: MPn
///        value pairs into the building speed multiplier slots.
///
/// The same parser as parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult, writing the building speed
/// array through MultiPlayMults::setBuildingSpeedMult. MP1 to MP8 are
/// accepted without regard to case; other names are ignored.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    The multiplier record that all five keys write to.
/// \param userData Not used.
/// \see parseMultiPlayUnitXPMult

/// \fn void parseHuman(INI *ini, void *instance, void *, const void *)
/// \brief Field parser for Human: reads Yes or No into the first flag of the
///        object being filled.
///
/// Human, Computer, LoadAIScripts, ForcePlayerTeam and AllowedFactions form
/// one five-row FieldParse table. Every row has offset zero, and the first
/// four parsers, documented here, write into the object being filled
/// (instance) at a fixed position of their own instead of using the field
/// address. Each reads its value into a local with one of the reader's
/// scalar parsers and then copies it into place: three one-byte flags
/// (Human, Computer, LoadAIScripts) followed by an integer
/// (ForcePlayerTeam). Which definition owns this table is not identified.
/// The function names are descriptive, taken from the BFME 1
/// reconstruction and the keys. Human's value is read with INI::parseBool,
/// which accepts Yes or No without regard to case.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance [out] The object being filled; receives the flag.
/// \see parseComputer, parseLoadAIScripts, parseForcePlayerTeam,
///      INI::parseBool

/// \fn void parseComputer(INI *ini, void *instance, void *, const void *)
/// \brief Field parser for Computer: reads Yes or No into the second flag of
///        the object being filled.
///
/// Read with INI::parseBool and stored in the object being filled, next to
/// the Human flag; see parseHuman for the table this belongs to.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance [out] The object being filled; receives the flag.
/// \see parseHuman

/// \fn void parseLoadAIScripts(INI *ini, void *instance, void *, const void *)
/// \brief Field parser for LoadAIScripts: reads Yes or No into the third
///        flag of the object being filled.
///
/// Read with INI::parseBool and stored in the object being filled, after
/// the Human and Computer flags; see parseHuman for the table this belongs
/// to.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance [out] The object being filled; receives the flag.
/// \see parseHuman

/// \fn void parseForcePlayerTeam(INI *ini, void *instance, void *, const void *)
/// \brief Field parser for ForcePlayerTeam: reads an integer into the object
///        being filled.
///
/// Read with INI::parseInt and stored in the object being filled, after
/// the three flags; see parseHuman for the table this belongs to.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance [out] The object being filled; receives the number.
/// \see parseHuman, INI::parseInt

/// \fn void parseReallyLowMHz(INI *ini)
/// \brief Parser for the top-level ReallyLowMHz keyword: stores a processor
///        speed threshold in the level-of-detail manager.
///
/// ReallyLowMHz is registered as a keyword of its own, like a block name,
/// so the line stands outside any block and has no END. The parser reads
/// one integer with INI::parseInt and stores it in TheGameLODManager. When
/// the manager does not exist yet, the value is read and dropped without a
/// message.
///
/// Zero Hour has the same keyword and parser. There the threshold defaults
/// to 400, GameLODManager::isReallyLowMHz reports a detected processor
/// speed below it, and its one use turns the shell map (the 3D scene behind
/// the main menu) off when the static detail level is applied.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the keyword.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// If BFME 2 uses this threshold as Zero Hour does (its BFME 2 reader is
/// not identified here), a port that keeps automatic detail selection will
/// want a different measure than clock speed.
/// \see parseAudioLowMHz

/// \fn void parseAudioLowMHz(INI *ini)
/// \brief Parser for the top-level AudioLowMHz keyword: stores a second
///        processor speed threshold in the level-of-detail manager.
///
/// The companion of parseReallyLowMHz: the same shape, storing into the
/// manager field that follows the ReallyLowMHz threshold, and also dropped
/// when TheGameLODManager does not exist yet. Zero Hour has no such
/// keyword; BFME 1 has it. What reads the value, and how it affects audio,
/// is not established here.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the keyword.
/// \see parseReallyLowMHz

/// \fn void parseDelay(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for an audio event's Delay: reads a minimum and a
///        maximum and stores them in order.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads two integers with INI::scanInt into consecutive integers at the
/// field address. If the first is larger, the two are swapped. Nothing else
/// is checked: negative values are accepted.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The pair of integers, minimum then maximum.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's parseDelay is a static in INIAudioEventInfo.cpp whose
///       row has offset zero; it stores into two members of the audio event
///       and only asserts, in debug builds, that the values are ordered and
///       not negative. In Zero Hour AudioEventRTS picks a delay between the
///       two, in milliseconds. BFME 2's row points at the pair directly and
///       the parser repairs the order instead.
/// \see parsePitchShift

/// \fn void parsePitchShift(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for an audio event's PitchShift and
///        PerFilePitchShift: reads a minimum and a maximum and stores them
///        in order.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads two reals with INI::scanReal into consecutive reals at the field
/// address and swaps them when the first is larger. The values are stored
/// as written.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The pair of reals, minimum then maximum.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's parsePitchShift treats the numbers as percentages and
///       stores 1 + value / 100 for each, after a debug-only check. The
///       BFME 2 parser applies no conversion; whether BFME 2 converts the
///       values later is not established by this body. PerFilePitchShift
///       is not a Zero Hour key.
/// \see parseDelay

/// \fn static void ParticleSystemTemplate::parseRGBColorKeyframe(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_ini_field_callbacks
/// \brief Field parser for a particle system's Color1 to Color8: a colour
///        and the frame at which the particle has it.
///
/// Each of the eight keys fills one colour keyframe of the particle system
/// template.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls INI::parseRGBColor for the colour and INI::parseUnsignedInt for the
/// frame. INI::parseRGBColor reads R, G and B sub-tokens as integers, stops
/// the load with "color value ... out of range (0..255)" for a component
/// outside 0 to 255, and stores each as a real from 0 to 1.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The template being filled; passed on.
/// \param store    [out] The colour keyframe: the colour, then the frame.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Write Color1 = R:r G:g B:b frame, each component an integer from 0 to
/// 255 and the frame number last. Zero Hour's parser is the same.
/// \see ParticleSystemTemplate::parseRandomKeyframe, INI::parseRGBColor

/// \fn static void ParticleSystemTemplate::parseRandomKeyframe(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_ini_field_callbacks
/// \brief Field parser for a particle system's Alpha1 to Alpha8: a random
///        range and the frame at which it applies.
///
/// Each of the eight keys fills one alpha keyframe of the particle system
/// template.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads low and high with INI::scanReal and the frame with
/// INI::scanUnsignedInt, then sets the keyframe's GameClientRandomVariable
/// to the range with GameClientRandomVariable::setRange and its default,
/// uniform distribution. The order of low and high is not checked.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The keyframe: a random variable, then the frame.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Write Alpha1 = 0.0 1.0 0: low, high, frame. The range is a client-side
/// random variable; in Zero Hour those draw from the client random stream,
/// not the logic one. Zero Hour's parser is the same.
/// \see ParticleSystemTemplate::parseRGBColorKeyframe,
///      GameClientRandomVariable::setRange

/// \fn void parseBannerCarrierPosition(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for a horde's BannerCarrierPosition: a unit type and
///        a position, appended to the horde's list of banner slots.
///
/// The key belongs to the horde container's FieldParse table, alongside
/// other keys including RankInfo, ComboHorde, LeaderPosition,
/// BannerCarriersAllowed and SplitHorde. Each occurrence adds one entry, so a horde lists one line
/// per banner carrier position.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates a new slot record, then reads with the colon separator set:
/// the keyword UnitType and a name, which is stored as text; the keyword
/// Pos and a position read by INI::parseCoord2D. It appends a pointer to the
/// record to the vector at the field address. A missing or different
/// keyword stops the load with "UnitType expected" or "'Pos' expected";
/// the record allocated for that line is not freed.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [in,out] The horde's vector of banner slots.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Write BannerCarrierPosition = UnitType:SomeUnit Pos:X:0 Y:-10. UnitType
/// and Pos are case-sensitive. The unit name is only stored here, so a
/// misspelt name is not reported while parsing.
/// \see parseHordeContainSplitResult, INI::parseCoord2D

/// \fn void parseHordeContainSplitResult(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for a horde's SplitHorde: what a horde splits into,
///        appended to the horde's list of split results.
///
/// The key belongs to the same horde container table as
/// BannerCarrierPosition. Each occurrence adds one entry. The function name
/// is descriptive and follows the BFME 1 reconstruction; the key is
/// SplitHorde.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Allocates a new record, then reads with the colon separator set: the
/// keyword SplitResult and a name, the keyword UnitType and a name, both
/// stored as text, and optionally the keyword RankNumber and a number read
/// with atoi. The rank is 0 when RankNumber is absent, and a different
/// token in that place is consumed and ignored. The record's pointer is
/// appended to the vector at the field address. The error messages do not
/// name the expected keywords: a missing SplitResult stops the load with
/// "'Target' expected" and a missing UnitType with "'Result' expected". The
/// record allocated for that line is not freed.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [in,out] The horde's vector of split results.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Write SplitHorde = SplitResult:SomeHorde UnitType:SomeUnit RankNumber:2.
///   The keywords are case-sensitive.
/// - RankNumber goes through atoi: no macro expansion, and text that is not
///   a number reads as 0.
/// - Names are only stored here, so a misspelt name is not reported while
///   parsing.
/// \see parseBannerCarrierPosition
